<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ethiopian–Adal War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1527">1527</a>–<a href="/years/1543">1543</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ethiopian-Adal War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ethiopian–Adal War was a conflict fought in 1527–1543 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi</a>, and the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>. Adal forces overran most of the highlands, but Portuguese intervention and Ahmad's death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wayna-daga">Battle of Wayna Daga</a> in 1543 ended the conquest, leaving both states exhausted.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ethiopian-adal-war.jpg" alt="Ethiopian–Adal War" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horniman Museum, London, UK. Photographic Rights The Bridgeman Art Library · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deaths_of_Gragn_and_Da_Gama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
